plugin-bridge-js
================

This file records third-party work this repository reimplements or derives
from, and the licence under which that is done. It is not a list of
dependencies — `package.json` is that. It exists
because some of this repository's code was written by *reading a published
program* rather than by using it, and that is a thing which has to be
attributed rather than merely permitted.

`contract/FOREIGN.md` §5.1 requires a converted plugin bundle to
carry its upstream author, licence and repository. This file is the same rule
pointing inward. `docs/adr/0004-compatibility-layer.md` §2.7 decides which
licences it may point at.


The Aniyomi base-class driver
-----------------------------

Files:

    client-web/src/lib/plugins/foreign/shims/aniyomi-entry.ts
    client-web/src/lib/plugins/foreign/shims/kotlin-runtime.ts
        (the `KOTLIN_MODELS` section)

An extension written for the Aniyomi client application is a *subclass*. Most
of what makes one work lives in the class it extends — `AnimeHttpSource` and
`ParsedAnimeHttpSource`, together with the models beside them — which is not in
the extension's own file. To run one of those extensions, this project has to
supply that class.

It is supplied by **reimplementation, from the published source**, of the
`source-api` module of the Aniyomi client application, as published by the
`miwayomi` project under the **Apache License, Version 2.0**. The behaviour
reimplemented is the driver half: which member is called in which order, what
each default does when a subclass declares no override, and what each refuses.
No Kotlin was copied; the reimplementation is TypeScript-emitted JavaScript
with different structure, different names for its internals, and a different
failure taxonomy (`contract/plugin-api/ABI.md` §5). What is *derived* is the
behaviour, and the Apache-2.0 licence permits that with attribution, which this
notice is.


Episode numbering
-----------------

File:

    client-web/src/lib/plugins/foreign/episode-recognition.ts

A port of `EpisodeRecognition.kt` from the Aniyomi client application, also
Apache-2.0.

It is a port of the behaviour, not a copy of the text: the Kotlin object is
re-expressed as one self-contained TypeScript function, because the same body
is also emitted as source into converted plugin bundles, which cannot import
anything. The algorithm — the patterns, their order, the `extra`/`omake`/
`special` and `.a`-through-`.i` fractions, and `-1` for "no number found" — is
reproduced exactly, and is pinned by tests written against the upstream
doc-comment examples rather than against ours. Agreeing with the rest of the
ecosystem about which episode is episode twelve is the entire reason to port it
rather than invent one.

No modification is claimed beyond that re-expression.


The preference framework
------------------------

File:

    client-web/src/lib/plugins/foreign/shims/kotlin-runtime.ts
        (the `KOTLIN_PREFS` section)

An extension in this format is configured through Android's preference
framework: it declares a `PreferenceScreen` and reads the values back through
`SharedPreferences`. Neither exists in a browser, and an extension that mentions
either could not be translated at all until this build supplied them.

They are supplied by **reimplementation, from the published source**, of the
`android-compat` module of the same `miwayomi` project, under the **Apache
License, Version 2.0** — `androidx/preference/Preference.kt`,
`android/content/SharedPreferences.kt` and
`android/content/res/CompatSharedPreferences.kt`. What is reproduced is the
*shape*: which types exist, which properties an extension assigns on each, and
what each getter answers when nothing is stored.

Everything behind that shape is this project's own and is deliberately
different. There are no widgets, because a plugin never draws UI
(`contract/plugin-api/ABI.md` §1) — the values come from a manifest `settings`
block the conversion derived and the host rendered. There is no file: the
upstream store persists to a `.properties` file, and this one layers a per-run
overlay over the host's settings and persists nothing, because a plugin does not
get to edit a screen it is not allowed to draw. The property *names* are
upstream's, because extensions assign them directly and renaming them would mean
rewriting every assignment site.

No Kotlin was copied.


